# AWS Elastic IP Provisioning with Terraform

This documentation outlines the Terraform configuration used to provision an AWS Elastic IP (EIP) for the Nautilus DevOps cloud migration initiative.

## Overview

As part of the phased migration of infrastructure to the AWS cloud, external access for specific workloads requires a dedicated Elastic IP address. This task uses Terraform to dynamically provision and tag the Elastic IP using variables.

---

## Configuration Files

### 1. `provider.tf`
Configures the required Terraform version and provider constraints for AWS.

```hcl
terraform {
  required_version = ">= 1.0.0"
  required_providers {
    aws = {
      source  = "hashicorp/aws"
      version = "~> 4.0"
    }
  }
}

provider "aws" {
  region = "us-east-1"
}
2. variables.tf
Defines the input variable KKE_eip to store the name of the Elastic IP resource.

Terraform
variable "KKE_eip" {
  type        = string
  description = "The name for the Elastic IP"
  default     = "nautilus-eip"
}
3. main.tf
Contains the resource definition for the AWS Elastic IP (aws_eip), referencing the variable for tagging.

Terraform
resource "aws_eip" "nautilus_eip" {
  domain = "vpc"

  tags = {
    Name = var.KKE_eip
  }
}
Execution & Deployment
Navigate to the Terraform workspace directory:

Bash
cd /home/bob/terraform
Initialize the working directory to download required provider plugins:

Bash
terraform init
Apply the configuration to create the resource:

Bash
terraform apply -auto-approve
